# The restore-drill receipt, written on the box by scripts/restore-drill.sh and read by
# `verify:production`'s restore-drill-age check. It belongs to one machine, and the `secrets` check
# requires the checkout's working tree to be clean — an untracked receipt would fail it.
.restore-drill

# Next regenerates this on every build and points it at a different types directory depending on
# whether `next dev` or `next build` ran last — so tracking it means `npm run build` dirties the
# working tree. That matters here rather than being untidy: scripts/deploy.sh refuses to run on a
# dirty tree and `verify:production`'s `secrets` check asserts a clean one, so a tracked copy makes
# every deploy after the first refuse. Next writes it back whenever it is missing.
packages/web/next-env.d.ts
